import ImageCaption from '../../../components/summer-reading/ImageCaption.astro';

const timelineEvents = [
  { year: 'pre-1850s', label: 'Umuofia governs itself through councils, age grades, and the egwugwu.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1857',      label: 'First Church Missionary Society base at Onitsha on the Niger.' },
  { year: '1884–85',   label: 'Berlin Conference partitions Africa. No Africans present.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1886',      label: 'Royal Niger Company chartered to rule and trade simultaneously.' },
  { year: '1900',      label: 'Crown dissolves the company; Protectorate of Southern Nigeria established.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1900–10s',  label: "Warrant chief system imposed across Igboland — the novel's colonial world.", highlight: true },
  { year: '1914',      label: 'Lugard amalgamates north and south into the Colony of Nigeria.' },
  { year: '1929',      label: "Women's War: tens of thousands rise against the warrant chief system." },
  { year: '1958',      label: 'Achebe publishes Things Fall Apart in London.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1960',      label: 'Nigerian independence.' },
];
---

<SummerLayout
  title="The World of the Novel — Things Fall Apart"
  section="tfa"
  spoke="world"
>
  <div class="sr-spoke-header">
    <span class="sr-spoke-header-eyebrow">Things Fall Apart  ·  Spoke 01 of 05</span>
    <h1 class="sr-spoke-header-title">The World of the Novel</h1>
    <p class="sr-spoke-header-deck">Umuofia in the decade before everything changed — pre-colonial Igboland on its own terms.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sr-spoke-layout">
    <div class="sr-spoke-prose">

      <p class="sr-lede"><em>Things Fall Apart</em> is set in the late nineteenth century in Igboland — the southeastern region of present-day Nigeria, in the territory of the lower Niger River. The novel follows Okonkwo, a man of considerable standing in the village of Umuofia, across the final years of his world's existence as an autonomous, self-governing community. By the time the novel ends, that world has been enclosed by British colonial administration. The novel was published in 1958, two years before Nigerian independence.</p>

      <h2>Where the Novel Is Set: Igboland</h2>

      <p>The Igbo homeland occupies the rainforest country of southeastern Nigeria, straddling the lower Niger River and its delta. Before European colonization, the Igbo were one of the most numerous peoples in West Africa, with a population estimated in the pre-colonial period at several million, today at over forty million. They were not a unified political nation. The Igbo lived in hundreds of autonomous village communities, connected by shared language, cultural practices, and religious beliefs, but without a single centralized government or ruler.</p>

      <p>This political organization — each village a sovereign republic, governing itself through councils of elders, age grades, and a meritocratic title system — was not a failure to develop centralized governance. It was a fully developed alternative to it. The significance of this distinction becomes clear when the British arrive and discover they have no chiefs to co-opt.</p>

      <ImageCaption
        src="/images/summer-reading/tfa/t1-igboland-map.jpg"
        alt="Map of Igboland showing the southeastern Nigeria region, the Niger River, and major towns"
        figureNumber={1}
        caption="Igboland, southeastern Nigeria. Umuofia is fictional but geographically situated in this territory near Onitsha — the region where the Church Missionary Society established its first Igbo presence in 1857."
        credit="Wikimedia Commons. Public domain."
        accent="tfa"
      />

      <p><strong>Umuofia</strong> — the novel's primary setting — is one of nine related villages that form a loose confederation. Umuofia is renowned for its military strength and for the power of its <em>agadi-nwayi</em>, the spirit of the old woman. It is "never beaten" in war, and its Oracle at Awka holds authority over the surrounding region. It is also, for the novel's purposes, representative: what happens to Umuofia is what happened across Igboland.</p>

      <h2>The Timeline: Pre-Colonial Igboland to 1958</h2>

      <div class="sr-timeline" data-timeline-reveal>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry" data-highlighted>
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">Before colonization</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Igbo communities governed themselves through village councils, age grades, and the meritocratic Ozo title system. Justice was administered by the egwugwu — men who embodied ancestral spirits — and by the Oracle of the Hills and the Caves. No single ruler held authority over more than a village. Each community was sovereign.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1857</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The Church Missionary Society opens its first permanent base at Onitsha, on the Niger River — the beginning of sustained Christian missionary presence in Igboland.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry" data-highlighted>
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1884–1885</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The Berlin Conference partitions Africa among European powers. Fourteen nations attend. No African representatives are present. Britain's dominance in the Niger region is formalized.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1886</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The Royal Niger Company receives a charter from the British Crown to administer the Niger area as a commercial enterprise. Trade, politics, and colonial expansion are conducted simultaneously by a private corporation.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry" data-highlighted>
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1900</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The British Crown dissolves the Royal Niger Company and assumes direct control. The Protectorate of Southern Nigeria is established, with British district commissioners replacing the company's agents.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry" data-highlighted>
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1900–1910s</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The British impose the warrant chief system across Igboland — appointing individuals as "chiefs" to administer colonial authority, regardless of whether those individuals hold any traditional standing in their communities. The system produces resentment, corruption, and eventually rebellion.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1914</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Frederick Lugard amalgamates the Protectorate of Southern Nigeria and the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria into a single colonial entity: the Colony and Protectorate of Nigeria.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1929</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The Women's War (Ogu Umunwanyi): tens of thousands of Igbo and Ibibio women rise up against the warrant chief system and a rumored British plan to tax women. The British suppress the uprising with force. The warrant chief system is eventually dismantled. <em>This event postdates the novel's setting but illustrates what the system produced.</em></div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry" data-highlighted>
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1958</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Chinua Achebe publishes <em>Things Fall Apart</em> in London. Nigerian independence follows two years later, in 1960. The novel appears at the precise moment when colonial rule is ending and the question of what it did — to African civilizations, to African self-understanding — becomes most urgent.</div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <script>
        {
          const timeline = document.querySelector('[data-timeline-reveal]');
          if (timeline) {
            const entries = Array.from(timeline.querySelectorAll('.sr-timeline-entry'));

            const obs = new IntersectionObserver((observations) => {
              observations.forEach(o => {
                if (!o.isIntersecting) return;
                o.target.classList.add('is-visible');
                obs.unobserve(o.target);
              });
            }, { threshold: 0.15, rootMargin: '0px 0px -30px 0px' });

            entries.forEach(e => obs.observe(e));
          }
        }
      </script>

      <p><em>The novel starts here.</em> Not in 1958, but in the pre-colonial world — the world that 1884 and 1900 would begin to dismantle. Achebe places the reader inside that world before showing what happens to it.</p>

      <ImageCaption
        src="/images/summer-reading/tfa/t1-bonny-chiefs.jpg"
        alt="Photograph of Bonny Chiefs alongside the Naval Commandant of the Bight of Biafra and Bight of Benin, Nigeria, 1896"
        figureNumber={2}
        caption="Chiefs of Bonny with the Naval Commandant of the Bight of Biafra and Bight of Benin, 1896. Bonny is a trading port in the eastern Niger Delta. This photograph was taken at the precise historical moment the novel's world is ending — formal British colonial administration over the Niger region was established four years later. The photograph's staging, with British and Nigerian figures posed together, encodes the asymmetry the novel traces: the encounter is framed as ceremonial exchange, not conquest."
        credit="Wikimedia Commons. Public domain."
        accent="tfa"
      />

      <h2>Achebe's Strategic Choice: The World Before</h2>

      <p>The most significant structural decision in <em>Things Fall Apart</em> is where Achebe begins.</p>

      <p>He does not begin with the arrival of the missionaries, the establishment of colonial administration or the District Commissioner and the warrant chiefs. He begins with Okonkwo throwing Amalinze the Cat in a wrestling match. He begins inside a fully functioning, internally coherent world — its governance, its cosmology, its economy, its social obligations — before any colonial presence has appeared.</p>

      <p>This temporal choice constitutes a deliberate literary argument. Readers of <em>Things Fall Apart</em> spend the majority of the novel inside a world that works before they see that world disrupted. By the time the missionaries appear, readers already understand what is being disrupted from the inside, not as outsider observers of an alien society.</p>

      <p>Chinua Achebe was explicit about why this mattered. He had grown up reading British novels about Africa — including Joyce Cary's <em>Mister Johnson</em> — in which Africa existed as backdrop for European narratives and Africans existed without interiority, without history, without fully realized culture. He had been taught, through colonial education, to identify with the white characters in those books, not with the African ones. <em>Things Fall Apart</em> was his correction: a novel in which an African civilization is the subject, not the setting, and in which that civilization's destruction is tragedy rather than progress.</p>

      <h2>The Novel's Three Parts</h2>

      <p><strong>Part I (Chapters 1–13)</strong> establishes Umuofia and Okonkwo's place within it. The pre-colonial world is rendered in full: its agricultural rhythms, its religious calendar, its judicial institutions, its gender dynamics, its internal tensions. This is the world the reader must understand before the novel's central event — Okonkwo's accidental killing of a clansman's son during a funeral — sends him into exile.</p>

      <p><strong>Part II (Chapters 14–19)</strong> follows Okonkwo's seven-year exile in his mother's village of Mbanta. While he is away, the missionaries arrive in Umuofia. The church establishes itself. The colonial administration extends its reach. Okonkwo returns to a village that is no longer the same village he left.</p>

      <p><strong>Part III (Chapters 20–25)</strong> returns Okonkwo to Umuofia in the full presence of colonial authority: the District Commissioner, the court, the warrant chiefs. Okonkwo finds no will in his community to resist. The novel ends with his death and with the District Commissioner's reflection that Okonkwo's story might make "a reasonable paragraph" in the book he plans to write: <em>The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger</em>.</p>

      <h2>The Epigraph: Yeats and What It Means</h2>

      <p>Achebe opens the novel with three lines from W. B. Yeats's 1919 poem "The Second Coming":</p>

      <blockquote class="sr-pullquote">
        <p><em>Turning and turning in the widening gyre</em><br />
        <em>The falcon cannot hear the falconer;</em><br />
        <em>Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold.</em></p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Yeats wrote the poem in the immediate aftermath of World War I as a vision of civilizational collapse, of order dissolving. The poem ends with a foreboding sense of the change produced by such a collapse: a "rough beast" that "slouches its way" towards the future. Achebe borrows the title and the image of the widening gyre of the falcon's flight, but recontextualizes them entirely as a framework for understanding the collapse of Okonkwo's world. In Yeats, the center that cannot hold is European civilization; the collapse is the crisis of Western modernity. In Achebe's novel, the center that cannot hold is Umuofia — an African civilization, fully realized, destroyed not by internal failure but by external force and coercion.</p>

      <p>The choice of a Yeats epigraph is itself an argument. Achebe is claiming the same literary and cultural authority for Igbo civilization's collapse that Yeats claimed for the collapse of the European order. The traditions speak to each other across the colonial divide. What falls apart in Achebe's novel is a world as complex and coherent as any Western society that Yeats was mourning.</p>

    </div>

